<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chief Joseph</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hinmatóowyalahtq̓it, Joseph the Younger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chief Joseph was a leader of the Wallowa band of the Nez Perce people of the Pacific Northwest. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government ordered his people onto a reservation in 1877, he and other leaders led some 800 Nez Perce on a fighting retreat of about 1,900 kilometres towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, surrendering in the Bear Paw Mountains just short of the border. Exiled with his people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> and later to Washington state, he spent the rest of his life seeking their return to the Wallowa valley.</p></div>
